---
/** How the depth map tool works. */
import { fileInfo } from '@/lib/ml/assets'
import { DEPTH_MODEL } from '@/lib/ml/depth/model'

const size = Math.round((fileInfo(DEPTH_MODEL)?.wire ?? 0) / 1e6)
---

<h2>How to use it</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Drop a photo. It stays on your device.</li>
  <li>The first time, the model downloads once ({size} MB). The depth map appears a few seconds later.</li>
  <li>Choose High detail for finer edges, or Invert if your software wants near as black.</li>
  <li>Download the map as a PNG at the size of your photo.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How it works</h2>
<p>
  The tool runs Depth Anything V2 (small), a vision transformer trained on millions of images to predict relative depth
  from a single photo (Apache 2.0 licence). The photo is scaled so its short side is 518 pixels (or 798 for High), both
  sides a multiple of 14, the size of the model's image patches. The model gives one value per pixel that grows as the
  surface gets nearer. The tool stretches those values to the range 0 to 255 and scales the map to the size of your photo.
</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Grey value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>255 (white)</td><td>The nearest point in the photo</td></tr>
    <tr><td>128</td><td>Halfway in the range of the photo</td></tr>
    <tr><td>0 (black)</td><td>The farthest point, often the sky</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The values are relative to the photo. Two maps of different photos cannot be compared, and the scale is not linear in metres.</p>
